@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# 10. Ticket assignment
+
+Date: 2025-10-20
+Status: Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Agents pick up tickets to work. The help desk needs to record who holds a ticket, list an agent's queue, and tell the
+agent when a ticket is assigned to them. Only tickets still being worked (open or in progress) should change assignee.
+
+## Decision
+
+We will store each ticket's assignee as `assignee`: the agent's name, or `null` when nobody holds it.
+
+- `PUT /tickets/:id/assignee` with `{"assignee": "<agent>"}` assigns the ticket; `{"assignee": null}` unassigns it.
+- An agent is a non-empty name of at most 64 characters, the same limits as a requester.
+- Only open and in-progress tickets can be assigned or unassigned; any other status is 409.
+- Assigning a ticket to the agent it already has is not a change: no audit entry, no notification, `updated` stays.
+- The assigned agent is notified (`ticket.assigned`) through the outbox.
+- `GET /tickets?assignee=` filters: a name keeps that agent's tickets; empty keeps the unassigned ones. A ticket must
+  match every filter given.
+- Every ticket the API answers includes `assignee`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The command-line listing shows each ticket's assignee.
+- A new notification type needs a template (`ticket.assigned`).
+- Tickets already in a data file have no `assignee`; they load as unassigned.
